For "a = student" part, what actually happening behind?
====================================================================================================
This line is the main confusion point in Java pass-by-value:
a = student;

To understand what happens behind the scenes, you must think in terms of
✅ stack
✅ heap
✅ reference values
✅ copying of reference (not object)

Let's go very deep step-by-step.

✅ Original code (focus part)
static void fun(Student a){
    Student student = new Student();
    student.id = 2;
    a = student;
}

Called from:

Student x = new Student();
x.id = 1;
fun(x);
✅ Step 1 — Object created in main
Student x = new Student();
x.id = 1;

Memory:

Heap:
object1 → Student(id=1)

Stack(main):
x → object1

Here x stores a reference value (address of object1).

Example (imaginary address):

x = 1001
✅ Step 2 — Call fun(x)
fun(x);

Java copies the reference value.

a = copy of x

Memory now:

Stack(main):
x → 1001 → object1

Stack(fun):
a → 1001 → object1

Important:

✔ same object
✔ different variables
✔ reference copied

✅ Step 3 — Create new object inside fun
Student student = new Student();
student.id = 2;

Memory:

Heap:
object1 → id=1
object2 → id=2

Stack(fun):
a → object1
student → object2

Example addresses:

object1 = 1001
object2 = 2002
a = 1001
student = 2002
✅ Step 4 — a = student

This line:

a = student;

Means:

copy value of student into a

NOT:

❌ not copying object
❌ not modifying object1
❌ not changing x

Only reference value changes.

Before:

a = 1001
student = 2002

After:

a = 2002
student = 2002

Memory now:

Stack(main):
x → 1001 → object1

Stack(fun):
a → 2002 → object2
student → 2002 → object2

So a stopped pointing to object1.

This is the key.

✅ Step 5 — fun() ends

Stack of fun removed.

Stack(main):
x → 1001 → object1

object2 has no reference → eligible for GC.

✅ Why x did not change

Because Java passed:

value of reference

NOT

reference itself

Meaning:

x ----copy----> a

NOT

x ----same variable---- a
✅ Real life analogy

Think like this:

x = address of house A

You give copy of address to friend:

a = address of house A

Then friend writes:

a = address of house B

Did your address change?

❌ No

Only friend's paper changed.

✅ Most important rule
a = student;

means

copy reference value

NOT

change original reference
✅ Interview one-line answer

a = student reassigns the local reference variable a to a new object.
It does not affect the original reference because Java passes object references by value.